---
/*
 * /clinical-documentation-automation/ — the pillar page for the documentation-automation
 * cluster.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS
 * Search Console, 28 days to 2026-09-11: seven closely-related queries — "clinical
 * documentation automation", "automated clinical documentation", "how to automate
 * clinical documentation", "benefits of automating clinical documentation" and
 * variants — drew ~240 impressions and zero clicks, all landing on a 2024 blog post
 * ranking accidentally at position 16.4. The post is about complex care management; it
 * was never written for these queries and cannot win them.
 *
 * That post now links here, so the cluster consolidates on a page built for it.
 *
 * The honest sections are the point, not a hedge. This category is full of vendor pages
 * that answer "what is it" with a sales pitch, and the audience is clinicians who have
 * already been sold to. "What it does not automate" and the evaluation criteria that do
 * not favour us are what make the rest worth reading.
 *
 * CLAIM RULES, same as the comparison pages: no SOC 2, no EHR write-back. Both appear
 * here only as honest limitations.
 */
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import CtaDark from '../../components/CtaDark.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is clinical documentation automation?',
    a: 'Any technology that reduces the manual work of producing the clinical record — dictation, templates and macros, ambient AI scribes that draft a note from the visit conversation, document intake that reads inbound faxes and records, and coding support. They are different layers solving different parts of the problem, and most practices end up using more than one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the benefits of automating clinical documentation?',
    a: 'Time returned outside clinic hours, lower administrative burden, and documentation that more accurately reflects the complexity of the visit — which matters for coding that survives review. The benefit that gets counted least is retrieval: a record that is structured as it is created is one you can actually find things in six months later.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do you automate clinical documentation?',
    a: 'Start with the highest-volume, most repetitive task rather than the hardest one, keep clinician review in the loop, and measure the time spent before you change anything so you can tell whether it worked. Most failed rollouts fail because they began with the exception cases or because nobody established a baseline.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does automated documentation remove the need for clinician review?',
    a: 'No, and any tool claiming otherwise is describing a liability rather than a feature. Automation handles capture and structure. Deciding what is clinically true, what matters, and what goes in the record remains the clinician’s, and every automated draft should be reviewed before it is signed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will an AI scribe write notes directly into my EHR?',
    a: 'Some products integrate with specific EHRs; many, including Stream, do not write back and copy the finished note over in one click instead. If automatic write-back is a hard requirement for you, ask vendors to demonstrate it in your EHR specifically rather than in general, because coverage varies enormously by system.',
  },
];
